# Fiscal Policy, the Budget, and Deficits

> Fiscal policy uses the government's budget — spending and taxes —
> to steer demand; its deficits measure how much of the steering is
> done on borrowed money.

[definition]
**Fiscal policy** is the use of government expenditure and taxation
to influence aggregate demand, output, and employment. It is decided
by the government (through the Union Budget), unlike monetary
policy, which belongs to the RBI.
[/definition]

## 1. Expansionary vs Contractionary

- **Expansionary fiscal policy** — higher spending and/or lower
  taxes to fight recession and unemployment; the risk is inflation
  and borrowing.
- **Contractionary fiscal policy** — lower spending and/or higher
  taxes to cool overheating demand.

By timing, policy is either **discretionary** (deliberate changes —
a stimulus package) or **automatic** (built-in stabilisers — tax
revenues fall and welfare spending rises in a downturn, cushioning
incomes without any new decision).

## 2. Structure of the Union Budget

| Receipts | Revenue (current income) | Capital (creates liability or reduces asset) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Type | Taxes (net to Centre), dividends, interest | Borrowings, loan recoveries, disinvestment |
| Expenditure | Revenue: salaries, subsidies, interest payments | Capital: assets — roads, machines, loans given |

Revenue expenditure maintains the government and its programmes;
capital expenditure builds assets that raise future capacity. The
budget is presented in two parts — Economic Survey a day earlier,
then the Finance Minister's budget speech on 1 February. The old
plan/non-plan classification was replaced by revenue/capital
groupings.

[info title="Revenue vs Capital — the test"]
Does the receipt create a liability (borrowing) or shrink an asset
(disinvestment)? Capital. Is it regular income or running expense?
Revenue. Interest *paid* is revenue expenditure though the loan
behind it was capital.
[/info]

## 3. The Three Deficits

@math
Revenue\ Deficit = Revenue\ Expenditure - Revenue\ Receipts
@/math

@math
Fiscal\ Deficit = Total\ Expenditure - Total\ Receipts\ (excluding\ borrowings)
@/math

@math
Primary\ Deficit = Fiscal\ Deficit - Interest\ Payments
@/math

| Deficit | What it tells you |
| --- | --- |
| Revenue deficit | Government borrowing to cover *running* costs |
| Fiscal deficit | Total borrowing need — the headline number |
| Primary deficit | Today's borrowing excluding the burden of *past* borrowing |

A zero primary deficit means all current borrowing merely services
old interest — no new profligacy, just legacy cost.

## 4. FRBM Act, 2003

The **Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act** committed the
Centre to fiscal discipline: eliminate the revenue deficit and bring
the fiscal deficit toward long-term targets, with annual statements
to Parliament. It includes an **escape clause** allowing temporary
deviation for defined emergencies — invoked for COVID-19 — with a
commitment to return to the glide path.

## 5. Finance Commission (Article 280)

Every five years, the **Finance Commission** recommends how the
Centre's divisible tax pool is shared with states (vertical
devolution), how it is distributed among states (horizontal sharing,
based on population, area, forest cover, income distance), and grants
for local bodies — the fiscal federalism referee.

