[example]
If total expenditure is ₹100 lakh crore and receipts other than
borrowing are ₹85 lakh crore, the fiscal deficit is ₹15 lakh crore.
If interest payments are ₹10 lakh crore, the primary deficit is
₹5 lakh crore — today's borrowing net of yesterday's debt service.
[/example]

## 6. Financing and Crowding Out

Deficits are financed by borrowing — from the market (G-secs), small
savings (National Small Savings Fund), or occasionally the RBI.
Heavy market borrowing can **crowd out** private investment by
raising interest rates, which is exactly why FRBM-type discipline
exists.

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | One-line memory |
| --- | --- |
| Expansionary fiscal | More spending, less tax — anti-recession |
| Automatic stabiliser | Taxes and transfers self-adjust with the cycle |
| Fiscal deficit | Total spending minus non-borrowed receipts |
| Primary deficit | Fiscal deficit minus interest payments |
| FRBM Act | 2003; targets plus escape clause |
| Finance Commission | Article 280; five-yearly tax devolution |
| Crowding out | Government borrowing raises rates, displacing private investment |

[summary]
Fiscal policy counter-cycles demand through the budget; deficits —
revenue, fiscal, primary — grade the borrowing behind it, the FRBM
framework disciplines it, and the Finance Commission divides the
tax pool between Centre and states. Capital expenditure is the
deficit that pays for itself.
[/summary]
